mer including a  trigger warning for those with current  or previous experiences with disordered  eating as always feel free to skip this  video if it's not supportive to your  journey and if you are new here hello  welcome um definitely be sure to  subscribe to this channel and ring that  little bell so that you never miss out  on an episode  alrighty folks let's dive in  first off the sweet potato staple is  giving me a bit of kpop diet ptsd  and if you watch my video right here  you'll know exactly what i mean  i have nothing against sweet potatoes i  love sweet potatoes and i love them for  breakfast because they're rich in  antioxidants and fiber  but there is a massive obsession in kind  of the k-pop diet culture with the whole  sweet potato diet which is a great  great example of the phrase too much of  a good thing thankfully i'm not just  seeing a sweet potato monomial here you  know we've got a little more variety  so jb's breakfast is certainly way more  adequate than like the typical k-pop  diet that i've reviewed i mean we've got  some protein here from the chicken as  well as some extra carbs and fiber from  the apple and sweet potato  it's definitely a very simple meal and i  would love to see maybe some healthy  fats in there so perhaps like a sauce  for the chicken or some nut butter with  the apples but i also know that the  typical kpop diet is quite low in fat  so we definitely just need to see how  the rest of the day unfolds  basically  so plum tea is actually a very popular  korean drink and it sounds super  delicious and like coffee i don't care  what your morning beverage ritual is as  long as it's not served with a side of  pseudoscience  but i will say that one in vitro study  did find that tea made from plum extract  may help to stimulate muscle protein  synthesis and inhibit protein  degradation and i will also say that  plums do contain a variety of organic  acids and antioxidants so yes there is  some nutrition to be had here  but i'll also note that this is research  done in a petri dish not actual humans  in real life so i wouldn't expect  anything magical to come from drinking a  cup of the stuff and while i've seen a  lot of online influencers extrapolating  some of these alleged health claims  i didn't actually see any bs from jb  himself so i'm happy to say nothing to  report here  trouble bankers  [Music]  i feel like oolong's pr team needs to  step it up a bit because green tea seems  to get like all the wellness woo fanfare  but it actually ranks quite similarly to  green and black tea in terms of its  antioxidant potential and it contains  roughly the same amount of caffeine as  green tea there's also a fair bit of  cohort based research suggesting that  habitually drinking oolong tea  may provide brain bone and heart health  benefits so if you like something that  is more or less kind of like a hybrid  between green and black tea  then oolong might be a really great  choice  from  and i tried to look up how to pronounce  that so i do apologize that it's not  perfect is a popular korean barbecue  dish that's basically marinated  pork in all the really good stuff like  soy sauce sesame oil garlic ginger and  one of my all-time favorite korean  ingredients gochujang  i am  obsessed and while i think a lot of my  north american followers watching may  think well where's the avocado toast or  protein pancakes for breakfast lemon  water i think it's a really good  reminder that most cultures don't  pigeonhole certain flavors or certain  foods into certain meals of the day  and there's actually no physiological  benefit to it either so for those of you  stuck in like a breakfast rut  maybe re-evaluate what foods you deem  appropriate for the morning this to me  looks like a super satisfying second  breakfast so we've got lots of protein  and some fat in the marinated pork and i  love that he's kind of revitalized  leftovers with his own little unique  spin  but i would love to see this paired with  some rice or maybe that morning sweet  potato for some energizing carbs and it  also would be very easy to like throw in  some leftover veggies as well while he's  refrying  [Music]  okay  listen we are all super busy and i also  know that the schedule of these kpop  artists is borderline inhumane so i know  that fitting in meals can often feel  like a task but given the energy demands  of rehearsals and performing  you gotta make eating a priority  and this applies even if you're just  sitting at your desk like i do all day  long think about it  if you have time to put makeup on or do  your hair or even work out or watch this  video right now  you have time to sit down to a meal and  while it's a bit hard to follow  i think what jb is saying here is that  taking the time to eat helps to ensure  that he eats the right amount and  doesn't overeat in like a desperate  state of  so this is why i always talk about the  gas gauge if we let ourselves get right  down on empty we tend to make kind of  frantic hasty decisions and it's hard  not to overeat but eating when we've  reached a moderate level of hunger  allows us to be more mindful with our  meals and land ourselves at a more  comfortable state of fullness  so if this video doesn't motivate you to  order some korean food tonight  i don't know what will so jb's go to's  for lunch are kimchi jjigae which is  like a kimchi stew as well as don jiang  jigae which is a stew made from soybean  paste and one thing that i love about  korean food is that not only is it super  super flavorful but it also incorporates  a lot of really nutritious fermented  foods like kimchi and soybean paste this  not only gives each dish a unique cake  of flavor but also offers some really  great gut healthy probiotics as well  there's also some research suggesting  that fermented foods actually help to  make nutrients from food more easily  absorbed by the body which to me is  really a win-win all around  now i didn't hear him state this but  usually korean food is served with ban  con i hope i'm pronouncing that right  but these are like little side dishes  like cooked rice and other veggies so  i'm assuming we're getting some  vegetables in there along with the carbs  from some rice  which typically complement the protein  in the stews and i also have to  acknowledge the guy does seem to use  some oil when he's kind of stir-frying  his meals because i just feel really  relieved that we're not dealing with the  typical k-pop fat-free oil phobia that i  commented on right here  number one  all right so  even though jb suggests he's not much of  a snacker  it's great that when he does decide that  he feels like a snack  he actually seems to talk about a lot of  these kind of like sweet foods like  chocolate and stuff like that  very joyously also i just want to say  that i know society has equated snacks  with like ultra processed or less  nutritious foods  but i really would like us to reclaim  that term to just basically mean like a  mini meal  yes snacks absolutely can include fun  foods but their purpose is really to  kind of carry you from one meal to the  next and that to me means focusing on  the hunger crushing combo  so if you want milk chocolate pair that  chocolate with some nuts or some yogurt  or maybe like throw it into my protein  oats for example like i did in my hunger  crushing combo video right here  okay so i'm definitely seeing a  preference towards takeout meals here  which i think is kind of how a lot of  our generation nowadays eats given our  busy lifestyles but restaurant and  takeout food is typically quite high in  sodium and saturated fat and a lot of  korean takeoff dishes do depend on like  high sodium broths lots of soy sauce and  soy based paste  also like north americans adults in  korea are on average consuming  significantly more sodium than what  health authorities do recommend so in  2012 south korea implemented a national  plan to reduce sodium intake to try to  get intake to 3 500 milligrams per day  by 2020 which is still about 170 more  than the recommended amount  now whether or not a low sodium diet is  actually that important for overall  health is currently up for debate  we know however that excess sodium can  increase the risk of stomach cancer and  high blood pressure but a large cochrane  review meta-analysis found that a low  salt diet didn't actually have a  significant impact on heart disease risk  or death  in other words it seems to benefit the  blood pressure numbers but not  necessarily the risk of dying from high  blood pressure  confusing i know what the hell are you  talking about  but that's why we can't really look at  any one health marker in isolation now  heart health aside another side effect  of getting too much sodium is bloating  and since in this clip he actually did  say that eating two slices of pizza  makes him feel very very full  i would just want to be mindful that a  lot of sodium may also cause a similar  uncomfortable feeling so i know he's  super busy but i would try to encourage  cooking a few meals a week at home or if  that's just totally not realistic just  trying to ensure that he's enjoying a  variety of potassium rich veggie based  ban con to help balance the sodium  levels in the body  [Music]  okay so i just love how jb attributes  his enjoyment of seafood from his love  of the ocean and fishing  this is such a great reminder that food  is so much more than just nutrients and  calories in fact research has even found  that food memories both positive and  negative are significantly associated  with food choices and our general  attitude and behaviors towards food and  that is really not surprising  um  [Music]  so obviously choosing water most often  is ideal which it sounds like jb does  and while yes we do have correlational  research linking sugar sweetened  beverages consumption to things like  obesity and chronic disease  it's very important to note that this is  correlational research it's not like you  have one soda and you get diagnosed with  diabetes or heart disease so i truly  believe that if you love an occasional  coke or sprite and that's what brings  you joy then you can absolutely work  this into your week in the context of a  balanced nutrient-rich diet without any  significant impact on your health it  doesn't need to be all or nothing  good  okay so these all sound delicious and  especially in the case of the yogurt  even relatively nutritious when it comes  to dessert but he did mention needing to  eat the yogurt from his parents even if  he doesn't want to which is actually not  really an approach that i recommend  parents take research suggests that  using pressure or implementing like the  clean plate club  generally actually results in picky  eating or in enjoying food less not more  so if you want to learn more about  evidence-based feeding strategies or  picky eating strategies definitely check  out my video right here  okay first off i'm so effing hungry now  after that little montage um but i gotta  say we've made it this far without  hearing the big d word  but alas  here we are  now generally speaking i am appreciative  that the meals he cited here don't set  off my like kpop die culture alarm bells  aside from occasionally skipping lunch  which we talked about and the virtually  fat-free first breakfast meal  and i will say that i appreciate that  his discussion around foods  feels pretty joyful and unrestricted but  i would be shocked if he didn't feel  some pressure by the kpop diet culture  to look a certain way  and since he did mention enjoying late  night foods when he's not dieting i  really want to remind everyone that  eating at night  doesn't inherently make you gain weight  eating calories in excess  any time of day causes weight gain also  eating four fried chicken legs over two  days that's just two pieces of fried  chicken that's like 400 calories that's  not a massive mukbang cheat day and if  you pair that with maybe some veggies  and some rice  you've got a normal size meal not  something to freak out about likewise  with the sashimi that's actually a  really nutrient-dense choice  so if you have forbidden foods that  you've got rules about and you want to  start to think of them in more morally  neutral terms  definitely watch my video on building  trigger foods into balanced meals right  here i think that'll give you a lot of  great ideas and how you can work those  into your everyday now with all that  said what are my thoughts on jb's diet  well despite a little seemingly  inevitable kpop diet influence overall  it sounds like jb approaches food from a  place of enjoyment and sunlight also  convenience rather than a place of  restriction or reductionist wellness  culture beliefs he seems to really enjoy  foods from every food group and doesn't  explicitly demonize anything or make  unsubstantiated claims about ingredients  or food components like we saw with this  other kpop idol that i reviewed  i also just love that he seems to  embrace the joyful nostalgic and  emotional roles of food in addition to  its physical and nutrient based roles if  i had any gentle nutrition tips for jb  it would be to try to prioritize home  cooking a little bit more  this would just ensure that he's getting  some extra fiber love in there and  balancing those sodium-rich meals with a  little bit more potassium-rich  vegetables  but otherwise i think he's got a really  good sense of what feels good to his  body and how much he needs to eat to  help fuel his very busy life  and on that note that is all that i have  for you guys today if you like this  video be sure to give it the thumbs up  leave me a comment below with who you'd  like to see review next subscribe to the  channel and i'll see you next time on  abby's kitchen bye  you

